.\"
.\" SUBSTRATE(7) - Substrate Native Personality Overview
.\"
.TH SUBSTRATE 7 "January 2026" "Substrate OS" "Miscellaneous"
.SH NAME
substrate \- Substrate native kernel personality overview
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Substrate operating system implements a Unix-like kernel with support for
multiple binary compatibility layers called "personalities". The
.B native
personality (also called "substrate") is the primary execution environment
designed specifically for Substrate.
.PP
The native personality uses a stack-based syscall ABI similar to FreeBSD,
where syscall arguments are passed on the stack rather than in registers
(as used by Linux i386).
.SH SYSCALL ABI
.SS Invocation
System calls are invoked via
.B int $0x80
with the syscall number in
.BR EAX .
Arguments are pushed onto the user stack in reverse order (rightmost first),
followed by a dummy return address placeholder.
.PP
.nf
    push arg3
    push arg2
    push arg1
    push $0           ; dummy return address
    mov eax, SYSCALL_NUMBER
    int $0x80
    add esp, 16       ; clean up stack
.fi
.SS Return Values
.IP \(bu 3
Success: Return value in
.BR EAX .
For 64-bit return values,
.B EDX
contains the high 32 bits.
.IP \(bu 3
Error: Negative errno value in
.BR EAX .
.SH SYSCALL TABLE
The following system calls are implemented in the native personality:
.PP
.TS
tab(;) allbox;
lb lb lb
r l l.
Number;Name;Description
1;exit;terminate the calling process
2;fork;create a child process
3;read;read from a file descriptor
4;write;write to a file descriptor
5;open;open a file
6;close;close a file descriptor
9;link;create a hard link
13;time;get time in seconds
14;mknod;create a filesystem node
19;lseek;reposition file offset
20;getpid;get process ID
21;mount;mount a filesystem
22;umount;unmount a filesystem
23;setuid;set user identity
24;getuid;get user identity
33;access;check access permissions
36;sync;synchronize filesystem caches
37;kill;send signal to process
39;mkdir;create a directory
40;rmdir;remove a directory
42;pipe;create a pipe
46;setgid;set group identity
47;getgid;get group identity
48;signal;ANSI C signal handling
49;geteuid;get effective user ID
50;getegid;get effective group ID
51;acct;enable/disable process accounting
54;ioctl;device control
63;dup2;duplicate a file descriptor
66;vfork;create child process (vfork)
106;stat;get file status
107;lstat;get file status (no symlink follow)
108;fstat;get file status by descriptor
11;execve;execute a program
122;uname;get system information
141;getdents;get directory entries
144;msync;synchronize memory with storage
162;nanosleep;high-resolution sleep
183;getcwd;get current working directory
241;pmap_stats;get pmap statistics (Substrate-specific)
455;thr_new;create new thread (BSD-style)
.TE
.SH ELF BRANDING
Native Substrate executables are identified by ELF branding:
.IP \(bu 3
.B e_ident[EI_OSABI]
= 64 (ELFOSABI_TESTUNIX / Substrate)
.PP
The
.BR brandelf (1)
utility can be used to set or query the ELF ABI:
.PP
.nf
    brandelf -t Substrate myprogram
.fi
.SH PERSONALITY DETECTION
The kernel detects the personality from the ELF header at exec time.
If
.B ELFOSABI
is 64 (Substrate/TesUnix), the native personality is selected.
Otherwise, Linux (3), FreeBSD (9), or SVR4 (0) personalities are used
as appropriate.
.SH DIFFERENCES FROM LINUX
.IP \(bu 3
Syscall arguments passed on stack, not in registers
.IP \(bu 3
.B struct termios
has NCCS=32 (Linux i386 has NCCS=19)
.IP \(bu 3
.B time_t
is 64-bit signed
.IP \(bu 3
Some syscall numbers differ from Linux
.SH DIFFERENCES FROM FREEBSD
.IP \(bu 3
Syscall numbers may differ
.IP \(bu 3
Thread creation uses
.BR thr_new (2)
with BSD-compatible parameters
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /proc/self/personality
(planned) Current process personality
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR exit (2),
.BR fork (2),
.BR read (2),
.BR write (2),
.BR open (2),
